<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/intermediate/volcanoes-and-igneous-processes">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Volcanoes are <strong>the planet releasing internal heat and building new crust</strong> —
    destructive and constructive at once. The plate-tectonics lesson gave you the map: plates
    diverge, converge and slide, and magma rises where they do. This lesson goes inside the
    process — what magma is, why some eruptions ooze and others obliterate, the rocks that
    record each one, and how volcanologists read a restless volcano well enough to evacuate a
    city before it erupts. Eruption style is predictable from magma chemistry, hazards are
    specific to eruption type, and modern monitoring turns catastrophes into managed events.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="magma chemistry, eruption styles, hazards, forecasting" widget="equationStep" config={volcCfg} />

  <h2>How magma is made: three melting tricks</h2>
  <p>
    The counterintuitive fact at the base of all volcanism: the mantle below the crust is not
    molten. It is solid rock, hot enough to flow slowly but under such pressure that its
    minerals cannot melt. Magma therefore exists only where something defeats the pressure,
    and there are exactly three ways. <strong>Decompression melting</strong>: lower the
    pressure and the melting point falls with it — where plates diverge (mid-ocean ridges) or
    a hot plume rises (Hawaii), mantle rock rises too, the pressure drops, and rock melts
    without any heat being added. This makes most of the world's basalt.
    <strong>Flux melting</strong>: add water and the melting point plummets — water molecules
    break up the mineral bonds the way salt melts ice. Subducting plates dehydrate at depth
    and their water rises into the mantle wedge above, melting it; this makes the sticky,
    gas-charged arc magmas of the Ring of Fire. <strong>Heat-transfer melting</strong>: hot
    magma injected into cooler crust simply melts its way upward, raising the silica content
    as it consumes continental rock — the route to rhyolite and granite. One physical system,
    three triggers, and the whole diversity of volcanic behaviour follows from which trigger
    fired.
  </p>

  <h2>Classifying igneous rocks: the record of cooling</h2>
  <p>
    Every igneous rock is frozen magma, and its two key properties — composition and cooling
    rate — are written in its texture. <strong>Mafic</strong> magmas (basaltic) are silica-poor
    (45–52% SiO₂), iron- and magnesium-rich, and run at 1,000–1,200 °C with the viscosity of
    warm honey; they cool fast at the surface into fine-grained <strong>basalt</strong>, the
    rock of every ocean floor. <strong>Felsic</strong> magmas (rhyolitic) are silica-rich
    (over ~68%), cooler (650–900 °C), and as viscous as cold asphalt; they erupt explosively
    or — more often — never erupt at all, cooling slowly at depth into coarse-crystalled
    <strong>granite</strong>, the backbone of the continents. The same magma can produce either
    name depending on where it freezes: rhyolite is granite's eruptive twin, gabbro is basalt's
    intrusive one. Slow cooling means large crystals; fast cooling means microscopic ones —
    the one-line key to all igneous identification. The oceanic crust is basalt and gabbro
    because the mantle partially melts that way; the continents are granite because repeated
    melting, fractionation and recycling have distilled the lightest, stickiest magma upward
    for four billion years.
  </p>

  <h2>Magma, viscosity and gases: why eruptions differ</h2>
  <p>
    The master variable is <strong>silica content</strong>. Silica tetrahedra link into chains
    and networks even in liquid rock, and the more silica, the more tangled the melt — the
    stickier it is. Viscosity controls everything downstream, and the second ingredient,
    dissolved <strong>volatiles</strong> (water vapour, CO₂, sulfur gases), controls the
    violence. Imagine a bottle of fizzy water: the gas stays dissolved while the pressure
    holds, and foams violently the instant the cap comes off. Magma does the same as it rises.
    Fluid basalt lets bubbles escape easily — gentle, effusive Hawaiian eruptions of fire
    fountains and rivers, as at Kīlauea. Sticky rhyolite or andesite traps its gas until
    pressure shatters the magma into fragments — explosive eruptions that launch ash columns
    tens of kilometres high. The eruption spectrum runs Hawaiian → Strombolian (rhythmic
    bursts) → Vulcanian (short violent blasts) → Plinian (sustained supersonic columns; the
    type Pliny the Younger described at Vesuvius in 79 CE, founding volcanology as a
    written science) → caldera-forming super-eruptions, the rarest and largest, where the
    volcano's roof collapses into its own emptied chamber. Yellowstone's last caldera-forming
    eruption, 640,000 years ago, ejected about 1,000 km³ of rock — twice the volume of Lake Erie.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "magma is liquid rock, like lava." Magma is mostly
    <em>molten crystal mush</em> — typically 30–60% suspended crystals, some dissolved gas,
    and melt — closer to a slush than a liquid, and its behaviour (including viscosity and
    eruption style) depends on how much of that crystal load it carries. "Lava" is just the
    name once it reaches the surface and starts losing its gas.</p>

  <h2>Volcano types: anatomy follows plumbing</h2>
  <p>
    Four architectures cover the world's volcanoes. <strong>Shield volcanoes</strong> —
    Mauna Loa, Kīlauea, Iceland's racks — are broad, gently sloping domes of runny basalt
    layer upon layer; Mauna Loa measured from its ocean floor base is taller than Everest.
    <strong>Stratovolcanoes</strong> (composite cones) — Fuji, Vesuvius, Mount St. Helens, the
    elegant symmetric cones of the Andes — alternate sticky lava flows with ash layers; they
    are steep, beautiful, and the most dangerous type because their sticky, gas-rich magma
    builds pressure. <strong>Cinder cones</strong> are small heaps of loose pyroclastic
    fragments around a vent — common, short-lived, Parícutin in Mexico grew a 400-metre cone
    in a single year from a farmer's field (1943). <strong>Caldera complexes</strong> —
    Yellowstone, Crater Lake, Toba — are collapse scars of the biggest eruptions, often
    resurgent and long-lived. The type is set by the magma, which is set by the tectonic
    setting — which is why the volcano map and the plate map are the same picture.
  </p>

  <h2>Plate settings: where and why</h2>
  <p>
    Three settings produce nearly all volcanism. <strong>Divergent boundaries</strong> — the
    mid-ocean ridge system — host the <em>majority</em> of Earth's volcanic output, entirely
    underwater and almost unwatched: basalt gushing as the plates part, building new oceanic
    crust along 65,000 km of ridge. <strong>Subduction zones</strong> make the explosive arcs:
    oceanic plate dives, releases water at depth, and that water flux-melts the mantle above —
    producing sticky, gas-rich andesite-rhyolite magma that rises through continental crust.
    This is the Pacific Ring of Fire, home to most of history's deadly eruptions.
    <strong>Hotspots</strong> are fixed mantle plumes that torch the plates sliding over them:
    Hawaii's island chain is the plume's footprint, each island ageing linearly downwind — a
    hotspot trail is a motion picture of plate motion. Yellowstone is the same phenomenon
    under continental crust, with continental magma chemistry and correspondingly worse
    manners. Flood-basalt provinces (the Deccan Traps, the Siberian Traps) are the extreme
    cousins — million-year pulses of lava volumes in the millions of cubic kilometres,
    implicated in mass extinctions.
  </p>

  <h2>Hazards: the specific killers</h2>
  <p>
    Lava is the hazard everybody pictures and almost nobody dies of — it moves slowly enough
    to outrun. The real killers are faster. <strong>Pyroclastic flows</strong>: avalanches of
    incandescent ash and gas at 100–700 km/h and 300–700 °C, hugging the ground and
    incinerating everything — they killed Pompeii's last residents and all 28,000 of Saint
    Pierre, Martinique, in minutes in 1902 (two survivors, one in a jail cell). <strong>Lahars</strong>
    — volcanic mudflows — need no eruption at all: loose ash plus rain or a melted glacier,
    moving at highway speed down river valleys; Nevado del Ruiz, Colombia, 1985, buried the
    town of Armero under lahars and killed about 23,000 people with warnings issued and
    unheeded. <strong>Ashfall</strong> collapses roofs, ruins engines and crops, and grounds
    aviation — the 2010 Eyjafjallajökull plume cancelled 100,000 flights across Europe.
    <strong>Gases</strong> (CO₂ suffocation in low-lying basins — Lake Nyos 1986 killed 1,700
    overnight; sulfur aerosols acidify rain) complete the list. Each hazard has its specific
    geography and its specific forecast, which is why hazard maps, not heroics, are modern
    volcanology's main product.
  </p>

  <h2>Forecasting: the Pinatubo standard</h2>
  <p>
    Volcanoes rarely erupt without warning; they <em>grow restless</em> before they erupt.
    Monitoring reads four signals: <strong>seismicity</strong> (magma cracking rock — swarms
    of small earthquakes rising toward the surface), <strong>deformation</strong> (the volcano
    inflates as magma fills it; InSAR satellites and tiltmeters resolve millimetres of bulge),
    <strong>gas chemistry</strong> (SO₂ output rising as fresh magma nears — measured by
    satellite and by spectrometer), and <strong>thermal/remote sensing</strong>. The pinnacle
    case: in April–June 1991, Pinatubo in the Philippines showed all four; forecasts predicted
    a major eruption within days; tens of thousands of people and the local US air base were
    evacuated. On 15 June the volcano erupted Plinian-scale — the second-largest of the 20th
    century — and while hundreds died, the evacuation is estimated to have saved 5,000–20,000
    lives. It remains the discipline's model: volcanology cannot stop an eruption, but it can
    move the people out of the way.
  </p>
  <p>
    The craft is in the uncertainty. Volcanic unrest does not always end in eruption — most
    intrusions stall at depth and cool — so forecasters walk a narrow line: alarm too often
    and the public stops listening (the "boy who cried wolf" failure mode), too late and the
    evacuation cannot happen (Armero's failure mode). Vesuvius today is the hardest version of
    the problem: three million people live on its flanks, its last eruption was 1944, and its
    typical precursor-to-eruption window (based on 1631–1944 behaviour) is measured in days to
    weeks — enough for a planned evacuation, only if the roads and the decisions are ready
    first. Forecasting, at its heart, is a social technology built on geophysical instruments.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Volcanoes also run the planet's long-term climate chemistry. <strong>Volcanic
    winters</strong> are the demonstrable large-scale experiment: Tambora's 1815 eruption
    injected so much sulfur into the stratosphere that 1816 became "the year without a summer"
    — snow in June in New England, failed harvests across Europe, famine and (some historians
    argue) the writing of Frankenstein during a rain-locked Geneva holiday. Pinatubo 1991
    cooled the whole planet about 0.5 °C for two years, the cleanest natural test of what
    stratospheric aerosols do — and the empirical backbone of the solar-geoengineering debate.
    The <strong>carbon cycle</strong> runs through volcanism too: arc volcanoes return
    subducted carbonate to the atmosphere, closing the loop between seafloor subduction and
    atmospheric CO₂ over geological time — the slow thermostat that (per the rock-cycle
    lesson) has kept Earth habitable while the Sun brightened. Look closer at the economics:
    geothermal energy rides the same heat (Iceland: about 30% of electricity and roughly 90%
    of heating from volcanic heat), volcanic ash weathers into some of the world's most fertile
    soil (the agriculture ladder meets it there), and diamonds, pumice and most sulfur are
    volcanic products. The hazard is local and occasional; the benefits are planetary and
    continuous — which is why civilisations keep returning to the flanks of volcanoes,
    reasonably, generation after generation.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more frontier: the deep biosphere and the deep water. Submarine volcanoes at the
    ridges host hydrothermal vents — black smokers at 350–400 °C — whose chemosynthetic
    ecosystems thrive without sunlight and which are the leading candidate stage for life's
    origin. And a significant share of Earth's surface water (and some argue most of it)
    arrived degassed from the interior by exactly the volcanic processes in this lesson —
    volcanoes did not just build the ground; they may have built the ocean, the atmosphere and
    the conditions for us.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Pliny the Younger's letters; Sigurdsson, Melting the Earth; USGS Volcano Hazards Program; the Pinatubo case (Newhall &amp; Punongbayan, 1996); OpenGeology, An Introduction to Geology, ch. 4.</p>
